course: dictionary
lesson: 7027
title: Part: a piece, a role, or to join in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word part has many meanings, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. When you read «Some children are adopted: they join a family that did not give birth to them, and they are just as much part of it», the clue is "belong to" and "family", so part means belonging to a group.
explain: A part is also a piece of something bigger. A slice is a part of a cake. In maths, two equal parts make a whole, so if a cake is cut into 2 equal parts, each one is a half.
explain: Part can be a verb too. To take part means to join in, as in «Everyone can take part in sports day». To part means to separate, so when friends part at the gate, they go different ways.
fact: A part is a piece of something bigger, like a slice of a cake.
fact: To take part means to join in with something.
fact: A part can also be one piece of a machine, such as a car part, or a role in a play or film.
fact: To part means to separate or go different ways, and a parting (UK) or part (USA) is the line in your hair.
fact: «Partly» means in part, not completely.
exercise: «Some children are adopted: they join a family that did not give birth to them, and they are just as much part of it.» What does part mean here? | A piece of a cake | A line in your hair | *A member who belongs to the family | Something that is broken
exercise: If a cake is cut into 2 equal parts, what is each part? | A crumb | *A half | Nothing at all | A whole
exercise: «Everyone can take part in sports day.» What does take part mean? | Watch from the car | Break into pieces | Leave early | *Join in
test: Dad says, «We need new parts for the car.» What is a part here? | A slice of pie | A role in a play | *A piece of a machine | A line in your hair
test: The two friends had to part at the gate. What does part mean? | *To separate and go different ways | To cook a meal | To join together | To sing a song
exam: In the UK, the line in your hair is called a parting; what is that line called in the USA? | partly | *part | parts | parting
